  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🎹 Dungeon Synth Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Project and album names for dungeon synth, from cold stone keeps to cosy forest hollows."
    },
    "intro": "Dungeon synth is music for imagined places: castles, crypts, forests and far-off realms, played on synths and keyboards, often recorded alone at home. The names do half the work of setting the scene. This **dungeon synth name generator** has names for projects and <a href=\"album-name-generator.html\">albums</a> in four styles, from <em>Throne of Mists</em> to <em>Lantern Vale</em>.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Where dungeon synth comes from",
        "content": "<p>The sound grew out of the 1990s black metal scene, when musicians made atmospheric keyboard side projects between metal albums. <strong>Mortiis</strong>, who had played bass in Emperor, is the name most often cited from those early years, and <strong>Summoning</strong> from Austria built their atmosphere around Tolkien.</p><p>The name came much later: \"dungeon synth\" was coined around 2011 on Andy Werth's blog of the same name, which gave a scattered scene a label to gather under. Since then it has grown into a large community, mostly releasing on Bandcamp and on cassette, with projects like <strong>Old Tower</strong> and <strong>Erang</strong> among its best known.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Four styles of name",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Dark and medieval</strong>: keeps, crypts and fortresses. <em>Gravenhelm</em>, <em>Sunless Throne</em>, <em>Ashen Vigil</em>. The classic sound.</li><li><strong>Nature and fantasy</strong>: forests, glades and old magic. <em>Lichenwood</em>, <em>Moonveil Glade</em>, <em>Elderglen</em>.</li><li><strong>Cosy (comfy synth)</strong>: the warmer, gentler offshoot, all hearths and hollows. <em>Hearthgrove</em>, <em>Candlewick Keep</em>, <em>Mellowbrook</em>.</li><li><strong>Cosmic</strong>: stars, voids and celestial realms. <em>Astral Haven</em>, <em>Nebula's Edge</em>, <em>Moonfall Haven</em>.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How dungeon synth projects are named",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>A place.</strong> Many projects are named as if they were a location: a tower, a keep, a realm. The music then becomes a visit there. <em>Old Tower</em> is a good example.</li><li><strong>A borrowed world.</strong> Tolkien is everywhere in the genre: the German project <em>Thangorodrim</em> takes its name from Morgoth's mountains. If you borrow, borrow deep cuts.</li><li><strong>An invented word.</strong> A single strange name (<em>Mortiis</em>, <em>Erang</em>) leaves the world entirely up to the music.</li><li><strong>Albums as chapters.</strong> Album titles often read like places or episodes in the same imagined world, so a project and its albums can share a map.</li></ul>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Before you release",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Search Bandcamp, Discogs and the streaming services</strong> for the name. The scene is large and full of keeps and towers, so a quick search saves a rename later.</li><li><strong>Check it's searchable.</strong> A name that's also a common phrase is hard for listeners to find; adding a word or an unusual spelling helps.</li><li><strong>Think about the cover.</strong> Dungeon synth is a visual genre (old maps, woodcuts, pixel art). A name that suggests an image makes the artwork easier.</li></ul>"
      }
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "What is dungeon synth?",
        "answer": "A genre of atmospheric, mostly instrumental synth music with a medieval or fantasy feel. It grew from the ambient side projects of 1990s black metal musicians, and the name was coined around 2011."
      },
      {
        "question": "What is comfy synth?",
        "answer": "A warmer, gentler offshoot of dungeon synth: cosy forests, hearths and villages instead of crypts and fortresses. Choose \"Cosy (comfy synth)\" for names in that style."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can I use these names for my project?",
        "answer": "Yes, they're free to use and you don't need to credit the site. Search Bandcamp, Discogs and the streaming services first to make sure nobody's using the name already."
      }
    ]
